TITLE_OVERRIDES = {'how-us-furniture-stores-cut-returns-with-ar': 'AR for Furniture Stores: How True-to-Size AR Cuts Expensive Returns', 'how-us-decor-stores-cut-returns-with-ar': 'AR for Home Decor Stores: How True-to-Size AR Cuts Returns', 'how-footwear-stores-cut-returns-with-ar': 'AR for Shoe Stores: How True-Size Views Cut Size Returns'}

DESC_OVERRIDES = {'how-us-furniture-stores-cut-returns-with-ar': 'AR for furniture stores, in plain words: why size returns happen, how true-to-size AR (Augmented Reality) views let shoppers see a sofa or a table live in their own room before they buy, and how a furniture store starts with the pieces that cause most returns.', 'how-us-decor-stores-cut-returns-with-ar': 'AR for decor stores, in plain words: why pieces come back, how true-to-size AR (Augmented Reality) views let shoppers see a lamp, a mirror or a picture live in their own room before they buy, and how a decor store starts with the pieces that cause most returns.', 'how-footwear-stores-cut-returns-with-ar': 'AR for shoe stores, in plain words: why size returns happen, how true-to-size AR (Augmented Reality) views let shoppers see a shoe at its real size against their own feet before they buy, and what AR honestly can and cannot fix.'}

BODY_OVERRIDES = {'how-us-furniture-stores-cut-returns-with-ar': '\n<p>A $1,400 sofa comes back. You pay return shipping, warehouse inspection, possible damage repair, and then you sell it at a discount as open-box, if it sells at all. One return can erase the profit of three good orders.</p>\n<p>Most furniture returns have the same root cause, and it is not quality. It is size. The customer could not tell how the piece would sit in their room, so they guessed. AR exists to end the guessing.</p>\n<h2>Why furniture returns are a size problem</h2>\n<p>Online furniture return rates run far higher than apparel or electronics, and "doesn\'t fit / doesn\'t look right in my space" is consistently among the top reasons. Photos and dimension charts ask shoppers to do spatial math in their heads. Most cannot, so they order, hope, and return.</p>\n<p>Every step of that loop costs you: two-way freight on bulky goods, restocking labour, damaged-item write-offs, and a customer who now trusts you less.</p>\n<h2>What true-to-size AR changes</h2>\n<p>True-to-size AR puts an AR (Augmented Reality) view of your exact product, built to its real measurements, into the shopper\'s own room through their phone camera. A 96-inch sofa appears 96 inches long next to their actual wall. The "will it fit?" question gets answered on the product page instead of in the returns queue.</p>\n<p>This is different from generic 3D viewers that spin a model on a white background. If the model is not true to scale and not shown in the customer\'s space, it does not prevent size returns.</p>\n<h2>What to demand from an AR solution</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>True scale, guaranteed:</strong> models built from your real dimensions, not approximations.</li>\n<li><strong>No app download:</strong> browser-based AR (iPhone Quick Look, Android Scene Viewer) so every shopper can use it, not just the motivated few.</li>\n<li><strong>Done for you:</strong> the provider builds the models and handles integration. You should not need a 3D team.</li>\n<li><strong>Fast to live:</strong> weeks of onboarding means weeks of preventable returns.</li>\n<li><strong>Honest pricing:</strong> a setup fee plus a flat monthly rate, no per-view surprises.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>How ARQR360 works for a furniture store</h2>\n<ol>\n<li>You send photos and real dimensions of your products, bestsellers first.</li>\n<li>We build your true-to-scale AR (Augmented Reality) views and AR catalogue in about seven days.</li>\n<li>Shoppers tap "View in Your Space" on your product page or scan your QR code, and see each piece at real size in their room.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>Try a live sample on your phone right now: our <a href="/novara">furniture catalogue demo</a> shows exactly what your customers would see.</p>\n<h2>How AR for a furniture store works, in practice</h2>\n<p>There is no project to run on your side. You send us photos of the pieces you want to start with, plus their real dimensions. We build a true-to-size AR (Augmented Reality) view for each one and send back a link and a QR code for every piece.</p>\n<p>No programming, no changes to your website needed. The link sits on the product page next to the price. The QR code goes on the shelf tag, on your packaging, in your shop, on social media, anywhere. Your buyer scans it and sees your product live in their own room, real size.</p>\n<p>Nothing needs installing at either end. The view opens in the phone browser, and the sofa on the screen is the size the real one will be against their wall. If you want to see what AR for furniture stores looks like when it is finished, walk through the <a href="/novara">Novara Living sample furniture catalogue</a> before you send a single photo. It is the same kind of catalogue we would build for your store.</p>\n<h2>The cheapest way to start</h2>\n<p>Send a photo of your bestselling product and we will build you one true-to-size AR model free. Put it on your own phone, walk around it, check the scale against your own furniture. If it does not impress you, you have lost nothing. If it does, you know exactly what it will do for your return rate.</p>\n<div class="faq">\n<h2>FAQ</h2>\n<h3>How much does furniture return shipping typically cost?</h3>\n<p>It varies by size and distance, but bulky furniture returns routinely cost $200 to $400+ in two-way freight alone, before inspection and restocking. Preventing even a few returns a month pays for AR many times over.</p>\n<h3>Will AR really reduce my return rate?</h3>\n<p>AR attacks the top cause of furniture returns: size and fit uncertainty. When shoppers confirm fit before buying, fewer orders come back for that reason. No tool eliminates all returns, but removing the guesswork removes the biggest driver.</p>\n<h3>Do I need to change my website?</h3>\n<p>No rebuild needed. ARQR360 adds a "View in Your Space" button to your existing product pages on Shopify, WooCommerce, and similar platforms.</p>\n</div>\n', 'how-us-decor-stores-cut-returns-with-ar': '<figure class="postimg"><img src="/blog/how-us-decor-stores-cut-returns-with-ar/images/hero.jpg" alt="Shopper holding a smartphone showing a ceramic table lamp in true-to-size AR in a warm living room" loading="lazy"><figcaption>The whole idea: the lamp at true size, in the shopper\'s own room, before they buy.</figcaption></figure>\n<p>Picture a home decor store owner in Denver. She sells table lamps, mirrors, vases, and wall art from one shop and a Shopify store. Her website traffic is fine. Her problem is the return pile. A customer buys a lamp that looked perfect online, sets it on their side table, decides it looks too big, and ships it back. She pays the return shipping. She loses the sale. Sometimes the piece comes back scratched.</p>\n<p>This is the most expensive line nobody talks about. Decor is sold on looks, and looks change from room to room. A product photo cannot show what the lamp does to your living room light at 8pm. A true-to-size AR view can.</p>\n<h2>Why decor returns happen</h2>\n<p>Most decor returns are not about quality. They are about wrong guesses. The customer guesses, and the room says no.</p>\n<p><strong>The scale illusion.</strong> A lamp shot against a white studio background looks modest. On your side table it dominates. The photo has no frame of reference. The customer\'s table does.</p>\n<p><strong>The proportion problem.</strong> A 24-inch mirror looks right in the photo. Against their 60-inch wall it looks lost. A piece of wall art sized for a sofa hangs over a chair and feels wrong. Customers cannot measure proportion from a product photo, so they guess.</p>\n<p><strong>The light shift.</strong> Brass looks warm and soft under the store lights. Under their kitchen bulbs it looks yellow and harsh. The product never changed. The light did.</p>\n<p>Each wrong guess costs real money: return shipping both ways, inspection time, and a piece that may never sell as new again.</p>\n<h2>What the shopper is really asking</h2>\n<p>Every decor product page gets the same unspoken question: will this fit my room? Text answers do not satisfy it. A dimensions table says the lamp is 18 inches tall. That tells the customer nothing about how 18 inches looks next to their armchair.</p>\n<p>AR answers the actual question. The customer points their phone at their side table and the lamp appears at its true size, in their light. They see it against their own furniture before they pay.</p>\n<figure class="postimg"><img src="/blog/how-us-decor-stores-cut-returns-with-ar/images/img1.jpg" alt="Shopper pointing a smartphone at a bare bedroom wall with a round mirror shown in true-to-size AR on the wall" loading="lazy"><figcaption>The customer points their phone at the wall and checks the true size in seconds.</figcaption></figure>\n<h2>How a store puts AR on its product pages</h2>\n<p>There are two practical routes.</p>\n<p>Route one is a Shopify AR viewer app. The apps add a 3D viewer to your product pages for a small monthly fee. They are viewers only. You supply every 3D model yourself, usually commissioned from 3D artists at roughly $100 to $500 per product. We worked the same maths in our <a href="/blog/ar-cost-small-decor-store-pricing">pricing breakdown for decor stores</a>. The software is cheap. The models are the project.</p>\n<p>Route two is done for you. You send photos of your bestselling pieces and their real listed dimensions. We produce the true-to-size AR (Augmented Reality) views from your photos and host them. No 3D team. No files to manage. ARQR360 works this way: $199 one-time setup and $19 a month, locked for life for the first 50 stores, with all the AR views included. About seven days from photos to a live catalogue.</p>\n<h2>Where to put the AR links</h2>\n<p>An AR view nobody opens does nothing. Put it where the buying decision happens.</p>\n<ul><li><strong>Next to the price.</strong> On every product page, a "see it in your room" link beside the add-to-cart button. That is where doubt lives.</li>\n<li><strong>On shelf tags in the shop.</strong> A QR code on the tag opens the same piece in AR. Browsing customers check it at home and come back decided.</li>\n<li><strong>In your emails and social posts.</strong> A lamp post with an AR link turns a like into a room check. That is the step before the sale.</li></ul>\n<figure class="postimg"><img src="/blog/how-us-decor-stores-cut-returns-with-ar/images/img2.jpg" alt="Real ceramic vase on a side table next to a phone showing the same vase in AR at identical true size" loading="lazy"><figcaption>AR against the real piece, same size, side by side.</figcaption></figure>\n<h2>What the first months look like</h2>\n<p>Picture the same Denver store, three months in. The "too big" returns slow down first, because those were pure size guesses and AR kills size guesses. Support emails asking "will it fit my wall" drop. Shoppers who open the AR view buy with the thing already seen in their room, so they come back less.</p>\n<p>One more number, and it is Shopify\'s own data, not ours: products with 3D or AR views convert about 94% better than products without. The mechanism is simple. Shoppers who can see the true size hesitate less, and confident shoppers return less.</p>\n<p>For the full menu of options, read <a href="/blog/best-ar-options-home-decor-stores">the best AR options for home decor stores</a>. For the money side, we worked the return maths in <a href="/blog/the-payback-maths-ar-small-decor-store">the payback maths for a small decor store</a>.</p>\n<h2>How AR for a decor store works, in practice</h2>\n<p>There is no project to run on your side. You send us photos of the pieces you want to start with, plus their real dimensions. We build a true-to-size AR (Augmented Reality) view for each one and send back a link and a QR code for every piece.</p>\n<p>No programming, no changes to your website needed. The link sits on the product page next to the price. The QR code goes on the shelf tag, on your packaging, in your shop, on social media, anywhere. Your buyer scans it and sees your product live in their own room, real size.</p>\n<p>Nothing needs installing at either end. The view opens in the phone browser, and the lamp or mirror on the screen is the size the real one will be on their table or wall. If you want to see what AR for home decor stores looks like when it is finished, walk through the <a href="/maison">Maison Ora sample decor catalogue</a> before you send a single photo. It is the same kind of catalogue we would build for your store.</p>\n<h2>The cheapest way to check if it works for your store</h2>\n<p>You do not have to commit to anything to find out. Send a photo of your bestselling lamp or mirror and we will build you one true-to-size AR view free. Put it on your own phone, check it against the real piece, and see if your customers would use it. If it does not convince you, you have spent nothing. Try the <a href="/maison">live decor demo</a> first: it opens in your phone browser, no install.</p>\n<div class="faq"><h2>FAQ</h2><h3>Do my customers need an app?</h3><p>Not with ARQR360. Each product gets a link and a QR code that opens the AR view in the phone browser. Try the <a href="/maison">live decor demo</a> on your own phone.</p><h3>How long before my decor pieces are live in AR?</h3><p>About seven days from your photos arriving. On the do-it-yourself route it depends on model turnaround plus your own integration work. Ask for a written timeline before you commit.</p><h3>What does AR cost a small decor store?</h3><p>It depends on who produces the AR views, which is the biggest cost on most routes. ARQR360 is flat and public: $199 one-time setup and $19 a month, locked for life for the first 50 stores, with all AR view production included. The first test piece is free.</p></div>', 'how-footwear-stores-cut-returns-with-ar': '\n<p>Picture a shoe store owner in Melbourne. She sells good leather boots online and from one small shop. Her return shelf is never empty. A customer orders a size 8, it pinches at the toe, back it comes. Another buys sneakers in the wrong width because the photos looked right. Every return costs her the shipping both ways, plus an opened box she now has to resell at a discount.</p>\n<p>Footwear is one of the hardest things to buy sight unseen. A sofa either fits the room or it does not. A shoe has to fit a foot that nobody measured properly. So shoe retailers watch AR try-on with real interest: if a customer can see the shoe on their own foot, in their own size, before they buy, fewer boxes come back.</p>\n<p>Here is what phone AR can actually do for a shoe store, and what it cannot. No hype, just the practical version.</p>\n<h2>What customers get</h2>\n<p>The idea is simple. A customer taps a link or scans a QR code on the product page, points the phone at their feet, and the shoe appears at true size. They can check the colour against their jeans, see how a chunky sneaker looks next to slim trousers, and pick a size with more confidence. The whole thing runs in the phone browser. No app download.</p>\n<p>This matters for shoes more than for furniture, because fit is visual and personal. A customer can tell in seconds whether a boot looks right on their foot. That is a decision they no longer have to guess from photos.</p>\n<h2>What AR cannot fix</h2>\n<p>Be honest about this. AR cannot tell a customer whether the shoe is comfortable. It cannot measure an arch or predict blisters. Anyone who tells you AR ends fit returns entirely is selling you something.</p>\n<p>What AR does fix is the look and the size confidence. Plenty of shoe returns are "wrong look" returns: the colour is off, the style does not work with the wardrobe, the shoe is bulkier than expected. Those are exactly the returns a true-to-size visual takes away.</p>\n<h2>The 3D model problem, and who solves it</h2>\n<p>Every shoe needs a true-to-size 3D model. This is the part that stops most stores. Building shoe models takes a 3D artist, real dimensions, and material work so the leather looks like leather. One model per shoe, per colourway, done properly.</p>\n<p>The honest options: build them yourself (slow and expensive), pay per model (fine for a test, painful across a whole catalogue), or use a service that builds them as part of the package. ARQR360 is the third option. You send photos and real dimensions of your shoes, bestsellers first, and we build the true-to-size models as part of the service. No 3D hire on your side, nothing to learn.</p>\n<h2>What it costs and how fast it goes live</h2>\n<p>ARQR360 is flat and public: $199 one-time setup, $19 a month, locked for life for the first 50 stores. No per-model fees, no per-view fees. Your catalogue goes live in about seven days. Each shoe gets a link and a QR code that opens the true-to-size try-on in the phone browser.</p>\n<p>Try the <a href="/corso">live footwear demo</a> on your own phone to see how it feels. For the returns angle in more detail, read <a href="/blog/how-us-furniture-stores-cut-returns-with-ar">how U.S. furniture stores cut expensive returns with true-to-size AR</a>. The returns logic is the same, the product is just smaller.</p>\n<h2>How AR for a shoe store works, in practice</h2>\n<p>There is no project to run on your side. You send us photos of the shoes you want to start with, plus their listed sizes. We build a true-to-size AR (Augmented Reality) view for each pair and send back a link and a QR code for every style.</p>\n<p>No programming, no changes to your website needed. The link sits on the product page next to the size selector. The QR code goes on the box, on the shelf tag, in your shop, on social media, anywhere. Your buyer scans it and sees the shoe at its true size, against their own feet, before they order.</p>\n<p>Nothing needs installing at either end. The view opens in the phone browser, and it gives the shopper a real size idea they can check against the shoes they already own. If you want to see what AR for shoe stores looks like when it is finished, walk through the <a href="/corso">Corso Footwear sample footwear catalogue</a> before you send a single photo. It is the same kind of catalogue we would build for your store.</p>\n<h2>The safest way to test it</h2>\n<p>You do not have to believe any pricing page. Send a photo of your bestselling shoe and we will build you one true-to-size AR model free. Put it on your own foot, check it against the real shoe, and decide with the thing in front of you. If it does not convince you, you have spent nothing.</p>\n<div class="faq">\n<h2>FAQ</h2>\n<h3>Do my customers need an app to try on shoes?</h3>\n<p>No. Each shoe gets a link and a QR code that opens the try-on in the phone browser. Try the <a href="/corso">live footwear demo</a> yourself and point your phone at your feet.</p>\n<h3>How accurate is the sizing?</h3>\n<p>Models are built true to size from your photos and real dimensions. The free test model is the way to check: try it on your own foot and compare it against the real shoe before you commit to anything.</p>\n<h3>How many shoe models do I get?</h3>\n<p>Your whole catalogue. ARQR360 does not charge per model. We build true-to-size AR (Augmented Reality) views of your shoes from your photos and dimensions as part of the service, and new stock gets the same treatment when your range changes.</p>\n</div>\n'}

